<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian National Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Congress movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian National Congress is a political party founded in 1885 that became the leading organisation of the Indian independence movement. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> it organised mass campaigns against British rule. Its struggle contributed to the end of British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress.jpg" alt="Indian National Congress" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original design of flag &amp; emblem by Indian National Congress.Vectorized version by Swapnil1101. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_National_Congress_hand_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was achieved through the mass civil disobedience campaigns led by Gandhi and the <b>Indian National Congress</b> and the growing impossibility of governing some 400 million people who no longer consented to be governed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Raj’s classification of Indians by caste and religion in the census and its separate electorates hardened communal identities, while the <b>Indian National Congress</b>, founded in 1885, and the All-India Muslim League, founded in 1906, developed into the parties that led India and Pakistan to independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of India was the outcome of a national movement that had challenged British rule since the founding of the <b>Indian National Congress</b> in 1885 and, under Mahatma Gandhi from 1920, mobilised millions through campaigns of non-cooperation and civil disobedience.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1885 seventy-two delegates met in Bombay for the first session of the <b>Indian National Congress</b>, encouraged by a retired British official, Allan Octavian Hume, and presided over by the Calcutta barrister Womesh Chunder Bonnerjee.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…as Giovanni Sartori distinguish the one-party system, where no other party may compete, from the hegemonic party system, where minor parties exist but can never win, and from the predominant-party system, where one party keeps winning free and competitive elections, as the <b>Indian National Congress</b> did after independence.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>…angered them, and the partial reversal in 1883 of the Ilbert Bill, which would have put Indian judges in Bengal on an equal footing with British ones, turned discontent into politics: in December 1885 seventy of them, many familiar with British political thought, founded the <b>Indian National Congress</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
